 To apply a Gaussian filter before the Sobel edge detection in MATLAB, you can use the `imgaussfilt` function. This function applies a Gaussian filter to the input image. Here's how you can modify your code to include a Gaussian filter:
```matlab
% Read input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);
% Apply Gaussian filter
gaussian_filter = imgaussfilt(gray_image, 3); % Standard deviation of 3 pixels
% Define Sobel operator in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];
% Apply Sobel operator in x and y directions
edge_image_x = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_x, 'same');
edge_image_y = conv2(double(gaussian_filter), sobel_y, 'same');
% Combine edges in both directions
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
% Display original image, edge image, and difference between them
figure;
subplot(1, 3, 1);
imshow(input_image); title('Original Image');
subplot(1, 3, 2);
imshow(gaussian_filter); title('Gaussian Filter');
subplot(1, 3, 3);
imshow(edge_image); title('Edge Image');
```
In this modified code, we first apply a Gaussian filter using the `imgaussfilt` function with a standard deviation of 3 pixels. We then apply the Sobel operator on the filtered image instead of the original image. Finally, we display the original image, the Gaussian filter, and the edge image for comparison.

